This is the most severe algorithmic issue in the codebase. `consolidateMemory` iterates every stored entry and calls `search()` for each one. Since `search()` is itself an O(k) linear scan over all embeddings, the whole operation is **O(k²)**:

```typescript
// Conceptually (from the implementation at lines 421-444):
for (const entry of allEntries) {          // O(k)
  const similar = await store.search(      // O(k) — full scan every time
    entry.embedding, limit, threshold
  );
  mergeEntries(entry, similar);
}
// Total: O(k²) — 1000 entries = 1,000,000 cosine similarity computations
```

**Fix:** Build a distance matrix once in a single O(k²) pass (unavoidable for exact search), or better, add a spatial index (the real HNSW implementation mentioned in the config) to reduce each lookup to O(log k):

```typescript
async consolidateMemory(threshold = 0.85): Promise<number> {
  // Single-pass: compare each pair once — O(k²/2) not O(k²)
  const entries = [...this.store.entries.values()].filter(e => e.embedding);
  const merged = new Set<string>();
  let count = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
    if (merged.has(entries[i].id)) continue;
    for (let j = i + 1; j < entries.length; j++) {
      if (merged.has(entries[j].id)) continue;
      const score = cosineSimilarity(entries[i].embedding!, entries[j].embedding!);
      if (score >= threshold) {
        mergeInto(entries[i], entries[j]);
        merged.add(entries[j].id);
        count++;
      }
    }
  }
  return count;
}
```

This halves the work and avoids re-scanning entries already merged.

---

Inter-agent communication uses `setInterval` polling rather than event-driven completion:

```typescript
// base-agent.ts lines 399-412 (conceptually)
async requestAgent(agentId, task): Promise<TaskResult> {
  await this.eventBus.emit('task.assigned', ...);

  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    const interval = setInterval(() => {   // ← polls every 100ms
      const result = this.stateManager.getTaskResult(task.id);
      if (result) {
        clearInterval(interval);
        resolve(result);
      }
    }, 100);
  });
}
```

With 15 agents potentially calling `requestAgent` concurrently, this creates up to 15 active polling intervals, each hitting `stateManager.getTaskResult()` 10 times per second. Under load (e.g., orchestrator routing 50 tasks) that's 500 state reads/second for work that could be zero-cost with events.

**Fix:** The `EventBus` already supports subscriptions — use it:

```typescript
async requestAgent<T>(agentId: AgentId, task: Task): Promise<TaskResult<T>> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const subId = this.eventBus.subscribe('task.completed', (event) => {
      if (event.payload.taskId !== task.id) return;
      this.eventBus.unsubscribe(subId);
      resolve(event.payload.result as TaskResult<T>);
    });

    const errorSubId = this.eventBus.subscribe('task.failed', (event) => {
      if (event.payload.taskId !== task.id) return;
      this.eventBus.unsubscribe(errorSubId);
      this.eventBus.unsubscribe(subId);
      reject(new Error(event.payload.error));
    });

    this.eventBus.emit('task.assigned', task.id, 'agent', { agentId, task });
  });
}
```

This eliminates all polling overhead and drops inter-agent latency from up to 100ms to effectively zero.

